# Policy Validator Functions Belong in `validators/*_validators.rego`

Validator functions and shared configuration (regex patterns, allowed-value
sets, `valid_*`/`contains_*` predicates, etc.) must live in a dedicated
`<scope>_validators.rego` file under the package's `validators/`
subdirectory — e.g. `policies/autodotes_policy/validators/ritm_validators.rego`
for RITM traceability checks,
`policies/autodotes_policy/validators/format_validators.rego` for generic
string/URL/HTML format validators.

- **Do not** define validator functions inline inside a policy's own
  `<name>.rego` file. That file should contain only its
  `<name>_violations` rules and the `<name>` entrypoint decision rule.
- **Reuse** an existing validator file when its scope already covers the
  new check (e.g. a new email-format check belongs in
  `format_validators.rego`, not a new file).
- **Only create a new `*_validators.rego` file** when no existing
  validator file's scope fits — e.g. the first check needed for a
  brand-new category of validation.
- Name the file after what it validates, not after the policy that first
  needed it (`format_validators.rego`, not
  `job_failure_notification_validators.rego`).

## Package and import rules

Every file under `validators/` declares a package one level deeper than its
parent policy package: `package <package>.validators` (e.g.
`package autodotes_policy.validators`). This is a sub-namespace, not the
same package as the policies — it is **not** automatically visible to
policy files.

- A policy file that needs a validator must explicitly
  `import data.<package>.validators` and reference functions/rules with the
  `validators.` prefix (e.g. `validators.ritm_violations`,
  `validators.valid_email(addr)`).
- **Only import `validators` in policy files that actually call into it.**
  Policies with no shared-validator dependency (e.g. one with entirely
  self-contained checks) must not add the import.
- Helpers like `input_extra_vars := object.get(input, "extra_vars", {})`
  are not validators — define them locally in each policy file that needs
  them (duplicating this one-liner across policy files in the same package
  is fine and expected; do not pull it in in via the `validators` import).

See
[validators/ritm_validators.rego](../../policies/autodotes_policy/validators/ritm_validators.rego)
and
[validators/format_validators.rego](../../policies/autodotes_policy/validators/format_validators.rego)
for the validator-side convention, and
[job_failure_notification.rego](../../policies/autodotes_policy/job_failure_notification.rego)
for an example of a policy importing and using them.
